"Two years before his death, my father gave me a small suitcase full of his writings, hand writings and notebooks."
Orhan Pamuk gave a speech called "My Father's Suitcase" when he received the Nobel Prize in Literature in December 2006. This emotional speech which sincerely conveys the spirit of Pamuk's thirty two years of writing effort, had a deep, worldwide impact. This book combines "My Father's Suitcase" which is a basic text about writing and living with Pamuk's two other speeches in which the same subjects and problems are discussed from other perspectives. "The Implied Author", the speech that Pamuk gave when he received the Puterbaugh Prize given by World Literature magazine, in April 2006 is about the psychology of writing and the urge and adventure of being a writer. Pamuk's other speech, "In Kars and in Frankfurt" that was given when he received the Peace Prize given by the German Publishers Associations in October 2005 is investigating the power of the writer to put himself in another's place and the political consequences of this very natural human talent. My Father's Suitcase consists of three speeches that are seen as a whole by their writer.
It's a unique, personal book on what writing is, how to become a writer, life and writing, the writer's patience and the secrets of the art of novel (from the author's website)"Î
ËLeyla Erbil'in zihinsel ozgurlugu, en basta, yapitlarinin, alisildik edebiyat turlerinin sinirlarini zorlama sonucunu dogurmustur. Genellikle oykucu ve romanci olarak taninsa da, Erbil'in yaygin kabul goren bu edebi turlerle, onlarin klasik formlariyla bir "sorunu" oldugu hemen her yapitinda fark edilir. Kabaca "roman", "oyku" diyebilsek de, turunu, yaslandigi gelenegi ilk anda tam belirleyemedigimiz, birbirini yinelemeyen, kendine ozgu yapitlar ortaya cikarmistir. Yine de, belli bir tanisikliktan sonra, bilmedigimiz bir metni elimize gecse, onun Erbil'e ait oldugunu rahatlikla anlariz. Tuhaf Bir Kadin'in onceleri bir oyku kitabi sanilmis olmasi bu baglamda ilginctir. Yazarin romanini "bitirmemesi" de onemlidir. Bu romanin yeni baskisinda yazar, okura, Mustafa Suphi'nin kaderiyle ilgili yeni kaynaklar ulastirir. Dikkat edilirse, tanimi geregi bitmis, yazarindan kopmus, okura firlatilip orada kalakalmis bir edebi turde, romanda yapilmaktadir bu guncelleme..."ü
ùO gun yeryuzunde kimse uyuyamadi.
Hemen hemen hic kimse.
Insomnia hastalarinin dahi arada biraz uyuduklari soylenir. Ama bu farkli.
Gecelerdir kimsenin gozune uyku girmiyor.
Bes gun daha boyle gecerse 'uyku mahrumiyeti psikozuyla bas basa kalacaklar.
Otuz iki gun sonra ise acili bir olum onlari bekliyor.
Hala uyuyabilen sansli azinlik artik o kadar sansli saymiyor kendini;
insanlar gruplara ayrilmak, olmek oldurmek icin, hicbir firsati kacirmiyor.
Adrian Barnes'tan, Arthur C. Clarke Odulu finalisti Uyuyamayanlar, insanin bilinc disina mercek tutan, gerilimin bir saniye bile dusmedigi essiz bir kurgu.
"Yilin en urpertici kitabi." - Slate
